\section{Hybrid Attack Vector: Keylogger Data + GPU-Accelerated Hashcat}

\subsection{Attack Description}

Captured keystroke logs are combined with GPU-accelerated password cracking using \texttt{Hashcat} configured for SHA-256 hashes. This hybrid attack uses the input patterns to prioritize candidate passwords and optimize cracking speed leveraging CUDA-enabled GPUs.

\subsection{Hashcat CUDA Kernel Parameters}

For SHA-256 cracking, the CUDA kernel parameters are tuned as follows:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{-w 4}: Workload profile for maximum GPU utilization.
  \item \texttt{-O}: Optimized kernel for performance (limited password length).
  \item \texttt{--gpu-accel=64}: Number of GPU threads per kernel launch.
  \item \texttt{--gpu-loops=1024}: Number of loops each thread performs.
  \item \texttt{--hash-type=1400}: SHA-256 hash mode.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Rainbow Table and Pattern Optimization}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Precompute rainbow tables for common password patterns extracted from keystroke logs.
  \item Use custom rules integrating leetspeak substitutions (e.g., \texttt{@→a}, \texttt{\$→s}) to generate password candidates.
  \item Integrate observed input timing patterns to infer probable character substitutions.
\end{itemize}

\section{Frequency-adaptive Markov Model for Leetspeak Resolution}

\subsection{Model Description}

A first-order Markov model $M$ is trained on the 2023 HaveIBeenPwned dataset to model character transition probabilities, adapted for common leetspeak substitutions.

\subsection{Preprocessing}

Define substitution mapping function $\phi$:
\[
\phi: \{ @ \to a,\, \$ \to s,\, 3 \to e,\, 1 \to l,\, 0 \to o,\, \ldots \}
\]

Apply $\phi$ to transform leetspeak variants to normalized forms before training.

\subsection{Training Algorithm Pseudocode}

\begin{verbatim}
Input: Dataset D of passwords (normalized with φ)
Output: Transition matrix T of size |Σ| x |Σ|

Initialize T[c1][c2] = 0 for all characters c1, c2 in alphabet Σ

for password in D:
    for i in range(len(password) - 1):
        c1 = password[i]
        c2 = password[i+1]
        T[c1][c2] += 1

for c1 in Σ:
    total = sum(T[c1][*])
    for c2 in Σ:
        if total > 0:
            T[c1][c2] /= total  # Normalize to probabilities
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Frequency Adaptation}

Update $T$ periodically with frequency counts from incoming keystroke logs weighted by recency:
\[
T^{(new)} = \alpha T^{(old)} + (1-\alpha) T^{(current)}
\]
where $\alpha \in [0,1]$ controls adaptation speed.

\subsection{Entropy Calculation}

For a password $P = p_1 p_2 \ldots p_n$, entropy $H$ under the Markov model:
\[
H(P) = - \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \log_2 P(p_{i+1} | p_i)
\]

Lower $H(P)$ indicates higher predictability.

