\subsection*{B. Hybrid Attack Workflow: CUDA-Optimized Hashcat Pipeline}

Our attack pipeline integrates intercepted password hashes from \texttt{/etc/shadow} with CUDA-accelerated brute forcing via Hashcat, optimized by warp sizing and kernel unrolling parameters tuned for Nvidia architectures.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Hash Extraction}: Parse intercepted authentication hashes, extracting salted SHA-512 hashes from \texttt{/etc/shadow} entries.
    \item \textbf{Preprocessing}: Normalize hashes, remove invalid entries, and prepare Hashcat input files.
    \item \textbf{CUDA Kernel Configuration}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Warp size set to 32 threads per warp to align with Nvidia warp execution model.
        \item Kernel unrolling factor set to 4, to balance register pressure and instruction throughput.
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Brute Force Execution}: Launch Hashcat with parameters:
    \begin{verbatim}
    hashcat -m 1800 -a 3 --kernel-accel=32 --kernel-loops=64 hashes.txt ?a?a?a?a?a?a?a?a?a?a?a?a
    \end{verbatim}
    where \texttt{-m 1800} corresponds to SHA-512 crypt hashes, and pattern matches 12-character mixed-mode passwords.
    \item \textbf{Postprocessing}: Validate cracked credentials and feed into lateral movement tools.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{C. Context-Aware Substitution Engine}

We propose a substitution engine based on Probabilistic Context-Free Grammars (PCFGs) trained on a 2023 Unix password corpus covering common admin password patterns. The engine incorporates Kolmogorov complexity metrics to prioritize pattern predictions with minimal description length.

\paragraph{Grammar Model:} Let $\mathcal{G} = (V, \Sigma, R, P)$ be a PCFG with variables $V$, terminals $\Sigma$, rules $R$, and probabilities $P$, where

\[
P: R \to [0,1], \quad \sum_{r \in R_A} P(r) = 1 \quad \forall A \in V
\]

\paragraph{Markov Chain Transition Matrix:} For modeling context transitions between password segments (e.g., word, digit, symbol), define states $S = \{w, d, s\}$ and transition probabilities $T$ as:

\[
T = \begin{bmatrix}
    P(w \to w) & P(w \to d) & P(w \to s) \\
    P(d \to w) & P(d \to d) & P(d \to s) \\
    P(s \to w) & P(s \to d) & P(s \to s)
\end{bmatrix}
\]

which is estimated empirically from the corpus.

\paragraph{Pseudocode:}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Context-Aware Substitution Engine}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Password pattern $p$, PCFG $\mathcal{G}$, transition matrix $T$, complexity threshold $\kappa$
\Ensure Ranked list of candidate passwords

\Function{GenerateCandidates}{$p, \mathcal{G}, T, \kappa$}
    \State Initialize empty candidate set $C$
    \State Parse $p$ into segments $S = (s_1, s_2, ..., s_n)$
    \ForAll{possible substitutions $sub_i$ for each segment $s_i$ according to $\mathcal{G}$}
        \State Compute transition probabilities $P_T = \prod_{i=1}^{n-1} T(s_i \to s_{i+1})$
        \State Construct candidate $c = sub_1 || sub_2 || \cdots || sub_n$
        \State Compute Kolmogorov complexity estimate $K(c)$
        \If{$K(c) < \kappa$}
            \State Add $(c, P_T, K(c))$ to $C$
        \EndIf
    \EndFor
    \State Sort $C$ by descending $P_T$ and ascending $K(c)$
    \State \Return $C$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Notes:}  
- Capitalization rules are modeled as context-dependent productions (e.g., first letter uppercase with probability $p=0.85$).  
- Numeric suffix increments follow a Markov chain with learned transition probabilities for digit increments.  
- Kolmogorov complexity $K(c)$ is approximated using compression-based methods (e.g., LZ77 compression ratio).

\subsection*{D. Performance Benchmarks: AWS g5.48xlarge vs. On-Premise Tesla V100 Cluster}

We evaluated the brute-force throughput for 12-character mixed-mode SHA-512 crypt hashes on two platforms:

